        <article class="article-content">

            <h1>Team</h1>
            <div class="meta-tags">
                <span class="tag">TryHackMe</span>
                <span class="tag">LFI</span>
                <span class="tag">LXD</span>
                <span class="tag">Dec 07, 2025</span>
            </div>

            <p>
                In this write-up, we tackle the "Team" room from TryHackMe. This challenge involves web enumeration, exploiting 
                Local File Inclusion (LFI) to steal SSH keys, lateral movement between users, and finally escalating privileges to root via LXD containers.
		<br>Link to the room: <a href="https://tryhackme.com/room/teamcw" target="_blank">https://tryhackme.com/room/teamcw</a>
	    
            </p>

            <h2 id="recon">1. Reconnaissance</h2>
            <p>
                We start with a standard Nmap scan to identify open ports and services.
            </p>
            <pre><code>$ sudo nmap -T4 10.82.165.94</code></pre>

            <img src="images_team/image-399.png" alt="Nmap Scan" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                The scan reveals three open ports:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>21/tcp:</strong> FTP</li>
                <li><strong>22/tcp:</strong> SSH</li>
                <li><strong>80/tcp:</strong> HTTP</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                I first tried to access the FTP server using <code>anonymous</code> login, but it failed.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-400.png" alt="FTP Failed" class="screenshot">

            <h2 id="web">2. Web Enumeration</h2>
            <p>
                Moving to port 80, I accessed the website via IP address. It displayed the default Apache2 Ubuntu page.
                However, inspecting the <strong>page source</strong> revealed a critical hint in the title tag.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-401.png" alt="Apache Default Page" class="screenshot">
            <img src="images_team/image-402.png" alt="Source Code Hint" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                The title said: <code>If you see this add 'team.thm' to your hosts!</code>.
                I added this entry to my <code>/etc/hosts</code> file to properly resolve the domain name.
            </p>
            <p>
                Visiting <code>http://team.thm</code> revealed a new website.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-403.png" alt="Team Website" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                I checked <code>robots.txt</code> and found a potential username: <strong>dale</strong>.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-404.png" alt="Robots.txt" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                I continued enumeration with <strong>Gobuster</strong> to find hidden directories.
            </p>
            <pre><code>$ gobuster dir -u http://team.thm/scripts -w ... -x php,txt,js,html</code></pre>

            <img src="images_team/image-405.png" alt="Gobuster Scan" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                Gobuster found <code>/scripts/script.txt</code>. Reading it, I noticed a comment saying the old version of the script 
                had credentials in it and was renamed.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-406.png" alt="Script.txt" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                Following this lead, I tried accessing <code>script.old</code> and found FTP credentials inside!
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-407.png" alt="Script.old Creds" class="screenshot">

            <h2 id="ftp">3. FTP Access</h2>
            <p>
                With the credentials found (<code>ftpuser</code>), I logged into the FTP server.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-408.png" alt="FTP Login" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                I found a file named <code>New_site.txt</code>. It contained information about a new development site under a subdomain 
                and mentioned that SSH keys (id_rsa) are placed in config files.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-409.png" alt="New Site Info" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                The note mentioned a subdomain. I added it to <code>/etc/hosts</code> 
                and accessed it.
            </p>

            <h2 id="lfi">4. LFI Exploitation</h2>
            <p>
                The dev site had a link that looked suspicious: <code>script.php?page=...</code>. This is a classic indicator of 
                <strong>Local File Inclusion (LFI)</strong> vulnerability.
            </p>
            <p>
                I confirmed the LFI by reading <code>/etc/passwd</code>.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-410.png" alt="LFI /etc/passwd" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                I also verified I could read <code>/home/dale/user.txt</code> directly via LFI.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-411.png" alt="LFI User Flag" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                Now the goal was to find the SSH private key mentioned earlier. The note said it's in a "config file".
                I created a wordlist of common config file locations and used <strong>Ffuf</strong> to fuzz the LFI parameter.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-412.png" alt="Wordlist" class="screenshot">
            <img src="images_team/image-413.png" alt="Ffuf Scan" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                Ffuf found a hit for <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code>. Wait, usually keys aren't there, but this is a CTF.
                Reading that file via LFI revealed the private SSH key for the user <strong>dale</strong> at the bottom of the config!
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-414.png" alt="SSH Key Found" class="screenshot">

            <h2 id="ssh">5. SSH & User Flag</h2>
            <p>
	    I copied the key to my machine, set correct permissions (<code>chmod 600</code>), and logged in as Dale. Remember to remove <code>#</code> from the key.
            </p>
            <pre><code>$ ssh dale@10.82.165.94 -i id_rsa</code></pre>

            <img src="images_team/image-415.png" alt="SSH Login" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                I checked <code>sudo -l</code> to see what Dale can do.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-416.png" alt="Sudo -l" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                Dale can run a script <code>/home/gyles/admin_checks</code> as the user <strong>gyles</strong> without a password.
                I analyzed the script. It reads user input into a variable <code>$error</code> and executes it! This is a command injection vulnerability.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-417.png" alt="Vulnerable Script" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                I exploited this by inputting <code>/bin/sh -i</code> when prompted for the date. This spawned a shell as <strong>gyles</strong>.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-418.png" alt="Lateral Movement" class="screenshot">

            <h2 id="privesc">6. Privilege Escalation</h2>
            <p>
                Now as Gyles, I transferred <strong>linpeas.sh</strong> to enumerate further.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-419.png" alt="Linpeas" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                Linpeas highlighted that Gyles belongs to the <strong>lxd</strong> group.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-420.png" alt="LXD Group" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                Being in the LXD group allows a user to create a privileged container and mount the host's root filesystem into it.
                I built an Alpine image on my attacker machine and transferred it to the target.
            </p>
            <img src="images_team/image-421.png" alt="Image Transfer" class="screenshot">
		<p>
                To exploit this, I first needed to build a compatible Alpine Linux image on my attacker machine:
            </p>
            <pre><code>$ git clone https://github.com/saghul/lxd-alpine-builder.git
$ cd lxd-alpine-builder
$ sudo ./build-alpine</code></pre>
	    <p>After building it, I transferred the <code>.tar.gz</code> file to the target machine using a Python HTTP server. Then, I executed the following commands on the target machine to import the image and initialize the container:
		<pre><code>$ lxc image import ./alpine-v3.13-x86_64-*.tar.gz --alias myimage
$ lxd init (accepted defaults)
$ lxc init myimage mycontainer -c security.privileged=true
$ lxc config device add mycontainer mydevice disk source=/ path=/mnt/root recursive=true
$ lxc start mycontainer
$ lxc exec mycontainer /bin/sh</code></pre>

            <img src="images_team/image-422.png" alt="LXD Exploit" class="screenshot">

            <p>
                Inside the container, I navigated to <code>/mnt/root/root</code> and successfully read the final flag!
            </p>
            
            <img src="images_team/image-423.png" alt="Root Flag" class="screenshot">
            
            <p>
                <strong>Root Flag:</strong> <code>THM{[REDACTED]}</code>
            </p>

        </article>
